WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.480
The SCP Foundation works very hard to keep
all knowledge and evidence of their discoveries

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:07.950
from reaching the outside world.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:12.830
As far as the average person is concerned,
the entities and anomalies documented by the

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:16.150
SCP Foundation do not exist.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:20.490
Anyone that stumbles upon an anomaly without
authorization will wind up having their memory

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:26.980
wiped with amnestics, or something far, far
worse, but either way, the knowledge never

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.890
enters into the general public’s consciousness.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:34.370
But what if there was an anomaly that even
the members of the Foundation didn’t know

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:35.370
about?

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:39.490
Not because they hadn’t discovered it yet,
but because, somehow, it wouldn’t let itself

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:40.570
be known?

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:45.620
It sounds impossible, but one particular Foundation
researcher faced this paradox while covering

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.920
an anomaly that is currently listed in the
archives without a number, referred to only

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.829
as: KNIFE.

00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:58.250
Junior Researcher Harris was assigned to SCP-4955
one day, and given the responsibility of examining

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.690
it and cataloguing any anomalous behavior
displayed by the item.

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:08.499
SCP-4955 was a simple knife of unknown make
and model, about the size and sharpness of

00:01:08.499 --> 00:01:10.159
a common kitchen knife.

00:01:10.159 --> 00:01:13.359
He took the knife into the lab, and ran the
usual tests.

00:01:13.359 --> 00:01:18.749
He scanned the materials, tested for any kind
of radiation, administered thermodynamic tests,

00:01:18.749 --> 00:01:23.039
and did everything else that is standard procedure
for a new SCP.

00:01:23.039 --> 00:01:27.100
In spite of all of his efforts, he found nothing
unusual about it at all.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.459
It was just a knife, with a metal blade and
a wooden handle.

00:01:30.459 --> 00:01:35.889
It didn’t give off radiation, it didn’t
have an unusual level of sharpness, it didn’t

00:01:35.889 --> 00:01:37.259
glow in the dark.

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:40.979
By all accounts, it was a completely ordinary,
boring knife.

00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:45.090
So why, then, was it even at the Foundation
in the first place?

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:50.200
Why was his time, which could be better spent
on other, more pressing research, being wasted

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:51.200
here?

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.290
Junior Researcher Harris decided to present
his highly uneventful report to his supervisor.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:58.479
It was then, finally, that the strangeness
began.

00:01:58.479 --> 00:02:02.570
At the start of the meeting, Harris’s supervisor
had no memory of anything being placed in

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:06.399
P-0055, where the knife was being stored.

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:09.360
Harris confirmed that, yes, there was something
in there.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.470
He continued his report, explaining that the
knife had displayed no anomalous properties

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:17.950
at all, and didn’t seem like the sort of
thing the Foundation should be wasting resources

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:18.950
on.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:23.360
As soon as he finished delivering his report,
his supervisor just stared at him, blankly.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.980
As if she was expecting something from him.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.640
Unsettled, Harris asked her if she was alright.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.950
She replied sharply, saying she was perfectly
fine but was getting tired of waiting for

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:35.130
him to give his report.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:39.380
Caught completely off guard, Harris gave her
his report on the knife a second time, only

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:41.840
to be met with the same blank stare.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:46.590
He attempted to give the report two more times
before giving up and leaving in exasperation.

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:51.500
Somehow, as soon as he told his supervisor
about the knife, she forgot everything he

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.390
said on the subject.

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:57.070
Determined to explore this bizarre turn of
events further, Harris set about recruiting

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:00.010
a colleague to help him conduct an experiment
with the knife.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:04.670
He managed to bribe another Foundation employee
named Researcher Smalls with five dollars

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:10.090
and the appeal of an unsolved mystery, and
he agreed to step in and participate.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:14.160
As soon as Smalls entered the room where the
research was being conducted, Harris held

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.690
up the knife and showed it to him.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:20.640
He asked Smalls, “Do you see this?” to
which Smalls replied, bafflingly, “See what?”

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.900
Harris approached Smalls, holding the knife
right up to his face.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:29.150
Still, Smalls insisted that he saw nothing
in the room aside from Harris and himself.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:33.420
The more Harris pressed him, the more Smalls
insisted, with a smile, that he did not see

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:36.170
a knife and had no idea what Harris was talking
about.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:40.670
In a frustrated outburst, Harris smacked Smalls
across the face with the broadside of the

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:41.670
knife.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:46.060
He stumbled for a moment, as the blow made
its impact, then steadied himself and looked

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:51.100
at Harris with the same expression as before,
the same expression he had seen on his supervisor

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.400
earlier that same day.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.830
Attempting to regain his composure, Harris
asked Smalls, “Did you feel that?”

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.950
Smalls grinned at him and replied, “All
I feel is bored.”

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:05.650
Determined to push the limits of his colleague’s
apparent forgetfulness, Harris cornered Smalls

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:08.130
and raised the knife to his face once more.

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:13.080
This time, slowly, maintaining eye contact
with him all the while, Harris used the blade

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.780
to make a deliberate, shallow cut across Smalls’
cheek.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:21.310
The man winced as the knife made its mark,
but as soon as the blade was tucked into Harris’s

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:24.450
pocket and out of sight, he did not reference
it once.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:29.820
He smirked at Harris, with blood dripping
down his cheek from a wound he would not acknowledge.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:34.470
This experiment with Smalls brought Harris’s
understanding of the knife into sharp perspective.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:38.820
The knife itself was not the anomaly, but
rather the knife’s effect on anyone who

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:39.960
observed it.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:44.460
Everyone, with the inexplicable exception
of Harris, who observed or learned about the

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.780
knife, was rendered somehow unable to verbally
acknowledge, or possibly even comprehend,

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:51.120
its existence.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.100
Harris and Smalls met for dinner later that
evening, and Harris was fascinated to see

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.030
that his colleague’s cheek was still smeared
with blood.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:02.530
It had been left to dry there all day, undisturbed
on Smalls’ face.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:06.310
Several other researchers noticed the wound
and asked Smalls about it, who insisted it

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:07.320
was nothing.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:12.380
The curious parties accepted his answer, but
only after looking at Harris for a long time.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:16.460
Though they would not say it out loud, on
some level they knew what had happened with

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:17.560
the knife.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:22.240
When Smalls wasn’t looking, Harris dosed
his drink with a strong memory recovery agent

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:27.030
developed by the Foundation’s antimemetics
team and attempted to introduce Smalls to

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:28.280
the knife again.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:33.530
Unfortunately, it had no effect, and Smalls
was still unable to acknowledge the existence

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:34.590
of the knife.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:39.830
The next day, determined to take his experimentation
to the next level, Harris cut his own cheek

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:40.889
with the knife.

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:46.040
He walked around the facility as usual, letting
his wound bleed freely onto his face and formerly

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.560
pristine white lab coat.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:52.860
Colleagues stopped him to ask what had happened,
and he told them plainly “I cut myself with

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:55.080
SCP-4955.”

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:59.210
Upon hearing those words, each person asking
about Harris’s injury immediately turned

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:01.640
and walked away, covering their mouth.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:06.330
Believing the knife to be some kind of antimeme,
or an anomaly that resists being recorded

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:09.490
or remembered, Harris administered further
tests.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:14.020
He ran the knife through a QNTM antimeme machine,
but found nothing.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:18.490
The knife behaved like previous antimemes,
but was unaffected by machinery designed to

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:23.200
observe them, and was immune to the effects
of memory recovery agents designed to counter

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:24.200
them.

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:25.560
What, then, was it?

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:30.320
Harris was beginning to feel like he was at
the center of a grand practical joke, and

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:31.660
he couldn’t figure out the punch line.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:37.590
All he knew was that his grip on reality was
beginning to slip, and the knife was to blame.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:43.550
Harris’s supervisor stopped by his office
to check in with him about SCP-4955, demanding

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:45.360
a report on the anomaly.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:49.150
At the end of his rope, he responded, “What
4955?”

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:53.370
Just as the supervisor began to reprimand
him for his poor attitude, Harris took the

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:59.250
paperwork for 4955 and shredded it into ribbons
with the knife as his supervisor watched.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:03.460
As soon as he was finished, he looked up to
see the supervisor staring at him, wide-eyed.

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:08.580
Slowly, she lifted her hand to her mouth…and
began to laugh.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:13.290
She laughed loudly, uproariously, like it
was the best joke she had ever heard.

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:17.870
Then, she asked him to take a look at SCP-3309
instead.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:22.540
Harris clenched his jaw, glared at her, and
lifted the knife to his hand.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:26.350
He sliced open the tip of his finger and flicked
the blood onto her face.

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:28.270
She didn’t even flinch.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:32.960
Instead, she turned away, laughing, face still
streaked with Harris’s blood, and went about

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:34.760
the rest of her day.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:38.120
This is the moment where Harris began to crack.

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:43.150
All he wanted was for someone, anyone, to
acknowledge the knife that was driving him

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:44.160
out of his mind.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:49.600
It was as if the entire facility, including
his own machinery, was gaslighting him.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:53.050
He decided to take more extreme measures to
get everybody’s attention.

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:56.740
At this point, whatever it took, he would
do it.

00:07:56.740 --> 00:08:01.620
Harris marched into the Foundation cafeteria
at lunchtime, carrying his usual bag.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:05.509
He walked into the center of the room, taking
in the sight of his colleagues.

00:08:05.509 --> 00:08:08.669
His supervisor still had his blood on her
face.

00:08:08.669 --> 00:08:12.670
Smalls still had a pool of dried blood on
his cheek from his day-old wound.

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:17.780
Harris took a deep breath and unzipped his
bag, pulling out a test rat from one of the

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:18.780
laboratories.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:22.820
As the crowded cafeteria looked on, Harris
pulled the knife from his pocket and dispatched

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:25.139
the rat with one quick slash.

00:08:25.139 --> 00:08:29.990
He held the dead rat up in the air, commanding
the attention of the room, then let it drop

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:31.680
with a sickening thud.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.680
No one said a word, they only watched and
quietly laughed amongst themselves.

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:38.810
Five days later, the rat was still there.

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:43.539
It had been stepped on and flattened by days
of foot traffic, fies were beginning to buzz

00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:47.850
around the corpse, and the smell had seeped
into the ceiling and walls.

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:50.680
Still, no one mentioned or referenced it.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:52.050
No one moved it.

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:54.570
And no one talked about the knife.

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:59.140
The following day, unable to stand the smell
or the sight of it anymore, Harris broke down

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:00.680
and cleaned up the rat.

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:03.810
As he did, he could hear people laughing behind
him.

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:07.890
Every time Harris entered a room, people were
laughing about something, and no one would

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:09.210
tell him what.

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:14.240
Still, when he brought up the knife, even
when he screamed and cried and pleaded, everyone

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:15.440
ignored the subject.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:19.550
It would not, and could not, be spoken about.

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:23.000
Several days later, Smalls complained that
someone had cut the plants he was growing

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:24.310
in his room.

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:27.410
Even though Harris had not cut his plants,
he decided to take credit.

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:28.410
“I did it.

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:30.260
With my knife,” he said.

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:34.800
As soon as the knife was mentioned, Smalls
sat down and went completely silent, abandoning

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:36.680
the subject entirely.

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:40.550
This gave Harris another crucial piece of
information on the nature of the knife’s

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:41.550
effects.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:45.790
It did not matter whether the knife was actually
present or involved in something, all that

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:49.010
mattered was the perception that it was.

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:52.610
Even the mention of the knife was enough to
invoke its effect.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:57.510
The days bled into each other, day after day
of Harris begging his colleagues to see what

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:02.200
he saw, demanding to know what the hell they
were laughing about, and feeling more and

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:03.840
more utterly alone.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.540
Harris’s mind had truly begun to come apart
at the seams.

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:12.020
In the second to last entry in his research
log, he simply wrote: “It’s almost show

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:13.020
time.

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:14.399
Time to give the people what they want.”

00:10:14.399 --> 00:10:18.880
The day after he wrote that cryptic entry,
Harris walked up to Smalls in the cafeteria

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:20.610
and tapped him on the shoulder.

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:24.420
When Smalls turned to greet his friend, Harris
stabbed him in the chest.

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:28.640
Smalls fell to the ground, curling up in the
fetal position, and Harris kicked the knife

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:31.170
deeper in as Smalls cowered there.

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:34.120
He did not stop until Smalls was dead.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:38.130
Harris was removed from the facility, leaving
the knife in Smalls’ body.

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:42.830
Harris was promptly terminated, eliminating
the last person who appeared was able to discuss

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:44.050
the knife.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:45.420
As for the knife now?

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:50.140
Its current location is unknown, making this
mundane little object one of the greatest

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:53.399
unsolved mysteries of the SCP Foundation.

00:10:53.399 --> 00:10:55.970
What was the knife, exactly?

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:58.470
What gave it such a strange hold on people?

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:00.910
In the end, it might not even matter.

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:02.800
After all, what is more frightening?

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:07.240
An object that prevents people from being
able to remember it, or being the only person

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:09.459
in the whole world who does?

00:11:09.459 --> 00:11:16.640
Now go check out “SCP-055 - Anti Meme / Unknown”
and “SCP-3999 - I Am At The Center of Everything

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:21.210
That Happens To Me” for more freaky anomalies
that can really get inside your head!

